WEBVTT

NOTE v2 English captions for Downward Facing Dog. Soft Jenny voice, phonetic Sanskrit.

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.954
This is Downward Facing Dog.

2
00:00:04.654 --> 00:00:09.790
In Sanskrit it is called ah doh moo kah shvah nah sah nah.

3
00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:16.402
Once more, slowly: ah doh moo kah shvah nah sah nah.

4
00:00:17.602 --> 00:00:21.010
Here is what this pose is for.

5
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:26.462
It can stretch the back of the legs and the shoulders.

6
00:00:27.012 --> 00:00:30.876
It helps make space along the spine.

7
00:00:31.426 --> 00:00:35.410
And it gently builds strength in the arms.

8
00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:42.910
Please be careful if your wrists, shoulders, or hamstrings feel tender.

9
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:48.790
You can bend the knees, or place a wedge under your hands.

10
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.474
Let's do it together, one step at a time.

11
00:00:55.474 --> 00:01:00.946
Step one. Come onto your hands and knees.

12
00:01:01.546 --> 00:01:10.258
Place your hands under your shoulders, and your knees under your hips. Spread your fingers wide.

13
00:01:11.158 --> 00:01:16.198
Step two. Tuck your toes under.

14
00:01:17.198 --> 00:01:25.934
Step three. Lift your knees, and send your hips up and back, like an upside-down V.

15
00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:35.574
Step four. Let your head hang softly, and press your heels gently toward the floor.

16
00:01:36.274 --> 00:01:40.042
It is fine if the heels stay lifted.

17
00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:44.690
Breathe in as you lengthen your spine.

18
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.782
Breathe out as you soften your shoulders.

19
00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:59.214
A common mistake: do not dump all your weight into your wrists, and do not force your heels down.

20
00:01:59.914 --> 00:02:04.090
Think hips up and back, not head down.

21
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:12.626
An easier version: keep a generous bend in your knees, and let your heels stay light.

22
00:02:13.326 --> 00:02:16.542
Make the back long first.

23
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:25.150
Hold for about thirty to sixty seconds, then lower your knees and sit back to rest.
